# Syntax: docs/LANGUAGE.md. Check with `rbb bid check`.
#
# No auction patterns here. These rules key off auction state that other
# modules set (trump agreed, game forcing), so they work after any auction
# that reaches that state.

module rkcb-1430 "Roman Keycard Blackwood (1430)"
  card   slam.blackwood.rkcb_1430
  needs  base

# ── The ask ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Rick's ruling:
#
#   Slam asks isn't about 18 -- what has partner shown? It's more about
#   33+ combined points, where in a suit contract the player with fewer
#   trumps gets to switch to short-suit points -- 1 for a doubleton, 3
#   for a singleton, 5 for a void, capped by number of trumps held. When
#   the slam asker has unprotected suits, we will normally use control
#   bids before keycard, and stop in game if there is a suit unprotected
#   in both hands.
#
# So the test is **33 combined support points with the agreed suit as
# trump**, `we.tp(trump)`: my exact count plus the range a raise recorded
# for partner. It replaces `tp(trump) + partner.hcp.max`, which was a
# workaround from before raises showed support points. The evidence
# behind the boundaries is in slam-catch.notes.md.
#
# Two branches, because partner's range is read from a different end.
#
# 1. **Partner is limited** (a raise, a limit raise) and nothing has
#    forced us to game. Partner has shown a top as well as a bottom, and
#    the only other call on offer is game, so the ask carries the whole
#    decision and 33 is measured against partner's maximum:
#    `we.tp(trump).max >= 33`. With it, the hand must play for twelve
#    tricks opposite a minimum (`losers <= 4`) and must not have **two
#    bare side suits** -- two or more cards with neither the ace nor the
#    king, where the first two tricks can go. That last test replaces
#    `controls >= 7`: it says which suit is the problem rather than
#    counting. One bare suit is allowed, because a raise facing it will
#    usually hold something there; two is where partner cannot cover
#    both. BBA draws the same line: over `1S P 2S P` and `1S P 3S P` on
#    the Basic-Bridge card it asks with none or one bare suit (272
#    boards) and never with two.
#
# 2. **We are already forced to game.** Partner's range has no top, so
#    `we.tp(trump).max` says nothing; what is known is partner's floor.
#    33 between us on that floor is the test, and where the trump
#    agreement recorded no support points for partner the fallback is my
#    own 18, a game force being worth about 13. (`we.tp(trump).min` would
#    be the way to write the first half, but the engine does not treat
#    `we.tp` as depending on my hand and drops the candidate before the
#    hand is looked at -- see slam-catch.notes.md, "For Rick".) Here the
#    ask ranks *behind* the system's descriptive calls: shortness, a
#    second suit and a control bid all say more than 4NT does, and the
#    ask is what is left when they are used up.
#
# When the card plays control bids (`slam.cue_bids.play`), a hand with a
# bare suit goes to `control-bids.bid` first and comes back here only
# once partner has shown a control.

when we.trump is suit, !asked, we.forcing = none | we.forcing = round
  4N  "Keycard ask in {trump}"
      when  we.tp(trump).max >= 33, losers <= 4     # 33 facing partner's maximum
      when  !((trump is not C, C>=2, !has(A,C), !has(K,C), trump is not D, D>=2, !has(A,D), !has(K,D)) | (trump is not C, C>=2, !has(A,C), !has(K,C), trump is not H, H>=2, !has(A,H), !has(K,H)) | (trump is not C, C>=2, !has(A,C), !has(K,C), trump is not S, S>=2, !has(A,S), !has(K,S)) | (trump is not D, D>=2, !has(A,D), !has(K,D), trump is not H, H>=2, !has(A,H), !has(K,H)) | (trump is not D, D>=2, !has(A,D), !has(K,D), trump is not S, S>=2, !has(A,S), !has(K,S)) | (trump is not H, H>=2, !has(A,H), !has(K,H), trump is not S, S>=2, !has(A,S), !has(K,S)))  # and no two bare side suits
      sets  ask=keycards(trump)
      artificial
      priority 1

when we.trump is suit, !asked, we.forcing = game
  4N  "Keycard ask in {trump}"
      when  tp(trump) + partner.tp(trump).min >= 33 | tp(trump) >= 18  # 33 on partner's floor, or 18 of my own
      sets  ask=keycards(trump)
      artificial

# After a control-bid exchange (`control-bids.bid`, when the card plays
# them). `asked control(t)` is partner's control bid; `answered control(u)`
# says partner was answering mine, so I am the hand that opened the
# dialogue and the one that takes charge. Above it, the stop-in-game rule
# at priority 20 has already ruled out a suit bare in both hands; below
# it, any control still to show outranks the ask.

when we.trump is suit, asked control(t), answered control(u)
  4N  "Keycard ask in {trump}"
      sets  ask=keycards(trump)
      artificial
      priority 5

# The answers (1430: 5C and 5D swapped relative to 0314).
when asked keycards(t)
  5C  "1 or 4 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 1|4  artificial
  5D  "0 or 3 keycards"                shows keycards(t) in 0|3  artificial
  5H  "2 keycards, no {t} queen"       shows keycards(t)=2, !has(Q,t)  artificial
  5S  "2 keycards and the {t} queen"   shows keycards(t)=2, has(Q,t)  artificial

# The asker places the contract. we.keycards(t) combines my exact count
# with partner's answer; the deck limit of 5 keycards resolves "1 or 4".
when answered keycards(t)
  7{t}  "Grand slam"
        when we.keycards(t)=5, has(Q,t) | partner.has(Q,t), grand_try
  6{t}  "Small slam: at most one keycard missing"
        when we.keycards(t)>=4
  P     "Sign off: partner's answer is in our suit"
        when partner.last=5{t}, we.keycards(t).max<=3
  5{t}  "Sign off: two keycards missing"
        when we.keycards(t).max<=3     # filtered out as illegal when the answer
                                       # is already 5{t}; P above covers that
        sets ask=keycard_correct(t)    # partner corrects only with 3-4
  5{t}  "Sign off: partner corrects to 6 with the higher count"
        when maybe we.keycards(t)>=4   # 0 or 3 / 1 or 4 still open
        sets ask=keycard_correct(t)

# When the answer was ambiguous the asker signs off in five, and the answerer
# corrects to six holding the higher count (3 or 4 keycards).
when asked keycard_correct(t)
  6{t}  "Corrects to six: the higher count"   shows keycards(t)>=3
  P     "Accepts the sign-off"

# TODO: queen ask (next step after 5C/5D), specific-king ask at 5NT.
